in münster people walk and cycle  everywhere this city might look historic  but in many ways it's also looking  forward co2 emissions have been  radically reduced here 10 years ago  Minster decided to close down its  coal-fired power station  the former plant is now quite literally  green 10 years after the phase-out of  coal-fired power the city is now also  decided to take its pension funds for  civil servants which were invested in  the coal industry and invest them  elsewhere the name of the game  divestment the investment initially  involves looking at financial  investments to see if they fulfil  sustainability criteria those might be  ecological criteria but they might also  be social criteria or criteria that  equates to good business management in  the broadest sense these two students  define divestment in rather more radical  terms they're the ones who put the  municipal authorities onto the idea it's  removing money from coal oil and gas the  two young women are part of a global  campaign that seeks to encourage public  and publicly minded organizations to  switch investments away from companies  linked to fossil fuels  Winston is the first German city to take  this step the activists tell city  authorities universities and foundations  about ways they can help stop climate  change many of the people that we speak  to are not aware that fossil fuel  commodities are no longer profitable and  the first argument that we encounter is  always a financial one  people say that they will take a big  loss if they withdraw their investments  but the executives in minsters municipal  authority have known for years that you  can make profits by investing in  sustainable energy rather than fossil  fuels they parted company with German  energy giant RWE years ago we started  out on this path in 2009 that's when we  sold our shares in our w/e a long time  before other local authorities began  selling their shares we were partly  motivated by financial reasons but also  by ecological ones you buddy activate  Edna Savi but that doesn't mean that  Minster is fossil free the coal bunker  at the former coal-fired power station  was converted to store waste heat from  electricity production electricity  produced by a gas-fired power plant but  the city is nonetheless managed to cut  carbon dioxide emissions in half their  local authority says that it can't  completely do without gas still the  city's treasurer's are surprised that  divestment is quite a simple process all  it requires is a chat with your fund  manager of course sometimes were  informed that we might incur higher  investment scheme fees or the like  problematic influence in the cities but  no one's informed us yet that it's not  possible so basically all cities could  follow suit yes
 

